{
  "status": "SUPPORTED",
  "reason": "WGCNA and related network‑analysis literature state that modules (clusters of co‑occurring features) can be summarized by module eigengenes that serve as optimal, lower‑dimensional summaries; this approach reduces multiple testing and yields reproducible, biologically coherent results compared with testing many individual features. (Langfelder & Horvath describe modules and module eigengenes as optimal summaries and note improved reproducibility/interpretability.)",
  "evidence_passages": [
    "“Weighted correlation network analysis (WGCNA) can be used for finding clusters (modules) of highly correlated genes, for summarizing such clusters using the module eigengene or an intramodular hub gene, for relating modules to one another and to external sample traits…”",
    "“Since the module eigengene is an optimal summary of the gene expression profiles of a given module, it is natural to correlate eigengenes with these traits.”"
  ],
  "sources": [
    "Langfelder P, Horvath S. WGCNA: an R package for weighted correlation network analysis. BMC Bioinformatics. 2008;9:559. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2631488/"
  ]
}

{
  "status": "AMBIGUOUS",
  "reason": "Method papers and reviews show micropollutants in real wastewaters are usually at ng·L⁻¹–µg·L⁻¹ and that analytical recoveries/imprecision improve above ~10 µg·L⁻¹, so researchers sometimes spike to tens of µg·L⁻¹ for detection/kinetics. However, practices vary (many studies use realistic, lower ng·L⁻¹ spikes or a range of concentrations), so the blanket claim that lab experiments often use single constant 10–100 µg·L⁻¹ spikes is only partly supported.",
  "evidence_passages": [
    "“Micropollutants are generally present in the nanogram per liter-to-microgram per liter concentration range in treated wastewater effluent.”",
    "“At OMP concentrations above 10 μg L–1, recoveries were within 20% of actual concentrations for simulated samples …”"
  ],
  "sources": [
    "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12302-024-00990-x (Schulze et al., guidance for the enrichment of micropollutants — notes environmental levels are ng·L⁻¹–µg·L⁻¹)",
    "https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acsestwater.3c00390 (Direct Measurement of Organic Micropollutants in Water and ... — notes analytical recovery improves above ~10 μg·L⁻¹)"
  ]
}

